  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nSurface high pressure continues to build in causing low level \nmoisture to dissipate, lake induced instability to wane, and low \nlevel trajectories to become more southerly. As lake enhanced rain \ndissipates early this morning, dry air takes over and we're in this \ndry pattern for the foreseeable future. This is as the upper jet \nretreats into MI and upper ridging builds into areas east of the \nRockies. An area of low pressure washes out between Wednesday and \nThursday as a cold front pushes southeastward ahead of another \nsurface high. The ECMWF has rain approaching the area, but that's \nreally the only model that does so. \n\nOtherwise, the temperatures are the most noteworthy piece of \nweather that should be discussed here. Morning temps fell back \ninto the 30s this morning, but lingering wind and limited \ndecoupling restricted frost formation to patchy at best despite \ntemperatures falling into the mid 30s and even some low 30s in \nsome outlying spots. 850 mb temps this morning reached around \n-2C and, even as that aforementioned return flow takes over, we \nhave our lowest temperatures of this young cool season as \nfreezing temperatures look to settle in along and south of \nUS-24. The give and take between surface high pressure-affected \nweak flow and encroaching strengthening gradient to our north \nand west once again lessens confidence on frost formation on the\nnorthern side of our freeze watch for Sunday AM. There are \nsigns in forecast soundings of a decoupling boundary layer Sun \nAM and again Mon AM, although lesser confidence in frost occurs \nMonday and even lesser for Tuesday. While WAA doesn't appear to \nbe established in the following days, temperatures are forecast \nto trend warmer, rising above 40 degrees by Wednesday AM. Highs \nsurpass 70 degrees again Tuesday morning, although a few areas \nin White, Pulaski, Cass, and Fulton could achieve 70F Monday as \nwell.",
